thing similar to what `get_description_with_cmd_summary` generates for `datalad --help`.  We should be able to pull out common bits from `datalad.cmdline.main`.\n\n    Running `help(datalad.api)` or `datalad.api?` or `pydoc datalad.api` would then show that function and its docstring, making it easily discoverable.\n\n  * In `datalad.__doc__`, point to `datalad.api` as the Python entry-point for datalad.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1533167985,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwOTc2MjY2OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2718#issuecomment-409762668"},"message":"\u003e I'll assume you're referring to the one-line command summaries shown by datalad --help.\n\nyes!\n\n\u003e introduces unnecessary complexity\n\nmay be for us, but not for the user.  Adding a non conventional function to api would add the complexity for user... I typically appreciate just being able to `?` things in ipython session and get good docs. E.g. try `numpy?`.  And since we are already loading all the modules/functions there should be little to no run-time impact to adjust the `__doc__`.\n\n\u003e In `datalad.__doc__`, point to `datalad.api` as the Python entry-point for datalad.\n\nyes, please","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1533180092,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwOTc5MzI4Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2718#issuecomment-409793283"},"message":"\u003e may be for us, but not for the user. \n\nYes, I'm considering the effect on the user in my opinion of whether the complexity is worth it.  Again, I don't think it is.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1533214197,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwOTkxNDcyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2718#issuecomment-409914724"},"message":"Never mind, I was stupidly thinking of the read-only docstrings of classes.  Modifying a module-level `__doc__` shouldn't add any complexity.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1533354416,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTc3MTEwOTM3MA=="},"status":2}]}